2... Bf5 3. e3 e6 ( 3... Nf6 { Transposes to 2...Nf6 3.e3 Bf5. } ) ( 3... Nc6 { Transposes to 2...Nc6 3.e3 Bf5. } ) ( 3... a6 4. c4 ) 4. c4 { Both sides have the same bishops, so White breaks the symmetry: the c-pawn challenges d5 and pulls Black's centre apart. } 4... Nf6 ( 4... dxc4 5. Bxc4 ) ( 4... Bb4+ 5. Nd2 ) ( 4... c6 5. Qb3 { Hits b7, which the bishop left when it went to f5. } 5... Qb6 ( 5... b6 6. cxd5 ) 6. c5 { Hits the queen. If it takes on b3, axb3 and the a-file opens for White's rook. } ) ( 4... Bd6 5. Bxd6 Qxd6 6. Qb3 ) ( 4... c5 5. cxd5 exd5 6. Qb3 ) ( 4... Nc6 5. Nc3 ) ( 4... Bxb1 5. Qa4+ ) 5. Nf3 Bd6 ( 5... c6 6. Qb3 ) ( 5... Bb4+ 6. Nbd2 ) ( 5... c5 6. cxd5 exd5 7. Bb5+ ) ( 5... dxc4 6. Bxc4 ) ( 5... Nc6 6. Nc3 { Transposes to 2...Nc6 3.e3 Bf5 4.c4 e6 5.Nc3 Nf6 6.Nf3. } ) 6. Bxd6 { Trade the bishop here: the pawn recapture ...cxd6 gives Black a worse pawn structure, while White keeps a free hand. } 6... cxd6 7. cxd5 { Opens the c-file and leaves Black with a tricky isolated d-pawn to look after. } 7... Nxd5 8. Nbd2 *
